# [What SHA-256 is]($heading.id('what'))

SHA-256 is a cryptographic hash function from the SHA-2 family. It turns any
input into a 32-byte digest. Changing one bit of the input changes about half
the bits of the digest. No one knows how to find two different inputs with
the same digest, or an input that produces a chosen digest. For practical
purposes the digest identifies the input.

# [Naming blocks by their content]($heading.id('cids'))

NomadFS stores everything as **blocks**: chunks of files, the blocks that
link chunks into a file, and directory listings. A block's name, its
**CID** (content identifier), is the SHA-256 hash of the block's bytes. Most
storage names data by *where* it is (a path, a disk offset). Content
addressing names it by *what* it is.

[Naming, checking and deduplicating blocks.](<$image.siteAsset('learn/content-addressing.svg').alt('Three panels. 1, naming a block: a tag byte 0x00 followed by the chunk bytes, 64 KiB to 1 MiB, goes through SHA-256 to give a 32-byte CID. 2, checking a block: a device wants CID X and sends GET_BLOCK X; a peer sends some bytes; they are hashed with SHA-256; if the result equals X the block is kept and cached, otherwise it is refused. 3, same bytes, same name: a.mov has chunks A, B, C and b.mov has A, B, D; the store holds A, B, C, D, four blocks rather than six.')>)

Every block starts with a one-byte tag that says what kind it is: `0x00` a
leaf (raw file bytes), `0x01` a parent (links to other blocks), `0x02` a
directory. The tag is covered by the hash, so a CID is the hash of the tag
and the content together. A chunk's CID is therefore *not* what `sha256sum`
prints for the chunk alone. Because every block carries its tag, a block
received from a peer can be decoded without any outside information.

On disk, the block store is a folder with one file per block, named by the
CID in hex. A block is written to `<cid>.tmp` and renamed into place, so a
crash never leaves a cut-off block under a valid name.

# [Checking every block]($heading.id('verify'))

The name doubles as a checksum. When a device asks a peer for block `X`
(`GET_BLOCK`), whatever comes back is hashed again before it is used:

- If the hash is `X`, the bytes are exactly the ones that were asked for.
  They go into the local block store and serve as a cache from then on.
- If not, the block is refused. A damaged or forged block can't get in
  under someone else's name. When reading the swarm folder NomadFS then asks
  the next device that might have the block.

The same check runs at other points: a block read from the local store is
re-hashed, so corruption on disk shows up as an error, not as wrong data.
Blocks pushed by a peer (`PUT_BLOCK`) are checked before they are stored.
Shared files are re-hashed as they are read from the original file (see the
[filestore]($link.page('learn/merkle-dag').ref('filestore'))). A file that
changed since its last scan reads as missing until the next scan.

This is why NomadFS can fetch a block from any device that has it, not only
the one that created it. You don't need to trust the source, because you can
check the bytes.

# [Storing data once]($heading.id('dedup'))

Two identical chunks have the same CID. Wherever they come from, the block
store keeps one copy: writing a block that already exists does nothing. Two
copies of a video in the swarm folder take the space of one. A file edited in
the middle shares every unchanged chunk with its earlier version, as long as
chunk boundaries don't move. Making sure they don't is the job of
[FastCDC]($link.page('learn/fastcdc')).

# [Names that commit to whole trees]($heading.id('trees'))

A parent block contains its children's CIDs, so its own CID depends on all of
them. The same is true one level up. The CID at the top of a folder pins down
every byte below it: if anything changes, the top CID changes. That is how a
share is published: one CID per share, recomputed on each scan. Comparing two
CIDs tells you whether two trees are identical, without reading them. The
[Merkle DAG]($link.page('learn/merkle-dag')) page shows the trees.

The DHT uses hashes too, but a different one: node and device IDs are
BLAKE2s-256 hashes of public keys (see [TCP]($link.page('learn/tcp').ref('ids'))).

Source: `src/storage/block.zig`, `src/storage/engine.zig`,
`src/storage/dag/codec.zig`, `src/fs/remote.zig`.
